    // A + C > B  =>  a + c + da + dc > b + db  =>  da + dc - db > b - a - c
    // B + C > A  =>  b + c + db + dc > a + da  =>  db + dc - da > a - b - c

    // This is a 3D counting problem with linear inequalities.
    // Given the constraints (up to 3*10^5), we need an efficient solution.

    // Let's think differently. 
    // Total number of ways to distribute at most l among 3 sticks is C(l+3, 3) = (l+1)(l+2)(l+3)/6.
    // We need to subtract the cases where the triangle inequality fails.
    // The triangle inequality fails if one side is >= sum of the other two.
    // Since the sides are positive, at most one of the three inequalities can fail (if A >= B+C, then B < B+C <= A, so B+C > A is false, but A+B > C and A+C > B are automatically true since A >= B+C implies A+B >= 2B+C > C and A+C >= 2C+B > B).
